SE: The 'strip\_tags' function is used to remove HTML and PHP tags from a string, but it does not sanitize attributes. In this case, 'original' and 'converted' variables are directly concatenated into HTML content without sanitation of their attributes.
BL: `$main_text .= '<div>' . '<a href="' . $original . '" target="_blank">' . $converted . '</a>' . '</div>'`;
BUG FOUND: YES.

SE: The 'strip\_tags' function is used to prevent code injection, but it's only used on the data that is later split into chunks of 100 characters. An attacker could potentially inject a closing tag within the first 100 characters of their input, breaking out of the split text function and injecting their own HTML.
BL: `$main_text .= '<div>' . $this->get_split_text(strip_tags($iv), 100) . '</div>'`;
BUG FOUND: YES. Although 'strip\_tags' is used, it does not fully prevent XSS attacks as the input is still being split into chunks and attackers can potentially inject their own HTML within the first 100 characters.

